DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       These  functions  calculate the complex argument (also called phase an‐
       gle) of _z, with a branch cut along the negative real axis.

       A complex number can be described by two real coordinates.  One may use
       rectangular coordinates and gets

           z = x + I * y

       where _x _= _c_r_e_a_l_(_z_) and _y _= _c_i_m_a_g_(_z_).

       Or one may use polar coordinates and gets

           z = r * cexp(I * a)

       where _r _= _c_a_b_s_(_z_) is the "radius", the "modulus", the absolute value of
       _z, and _a _= _c_a_r_g_(_z_) is the "phase angle", the argument of _z.

       One has:

           tan(carg(z)) = cimag(z) / creal(z)

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       The return value is in the range of [-pi,pi].

